{0 Stories}

A story is a named example of a component: a name, the source that a reader
sees, the controls that change it, and a function that renders it. A
component browser shows the stories of each component. The site of Silica has
one at [/components]. [Silica_ui.Story] is the type, and an app uses it to
write stories of its own components.

{1:type The story type}

{t
| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| [name] | the heading of the story |
| [source] | the code that the reader sees |
| [controls] | the controls that change the story |
| [render] | [~id values] gives the node of the story |
}

[render] is polymorphic in the event type, so the browser can put a story in
any view. [id] is unique in the page: pass it, or ids made from it, to the
components, so that one story can show more than once, for example once in
each theme.

{1:write Write a story}

Put the [render] function of each story in its own file. This one shows a
checkbox of silica-ui with the values of three controls:

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_checkbox_story.mlx[
open Silica_ui

let render ~id values =
  Checkbox.make
    (Checkbox.props ~id
       ~label:(Story.string values "label")
       ~checked:(Story.bool values "checked")
       ~disabled:(Story.bool values "disabled")
       ~classes:Style.checkbox ()
    )
]}

The story gives the controls and takes the source text from the file:

{@ocaml file=examples/shared/doc_stories.ml[
open Silica_ui

let checkbox =
  {
    Story.name = "Checkbox";
    source = Doc_checkbox_story_source.text;
    controls =
      [
        Story.choice "label" ~label:"Label"
          [ ("Accept the terms", "Terms"); ("Send me the news", "News") ]
          "Accept the terms";
        Story.toggle "checked" ~label:"Checked" false;
        Story.toggle "disabled" ~label:"Disabled" false;
      ];
    render = Doc_checkbox_story.render;
  }
]}

Keep the source honest: do not copy the code into a string by hand. A dune
rule writes the text of the file into a module, so the code that the reader
sees is the code that compiles:

From [docs/examples/native/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(rule
 (target doc_checkbox_story_source.ml)
 (deps doc_checkbox_story.mlx)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   %{target}
   (progn
    (echo "let text = {|")
    (cat %{deps})
    (echo "|}\n")))))
]}

{1:controls Controls and values}

A control is a toggle ([Story.toggle name ~label default]) or a choice from a
list ([Story.choice name ~label options default]). An option of a choice is a
value and its label. The values of a story are plain data, compared with
[=]:

{@ocaml[
# let values = Silica_ui.Story.defaults Doc_stories.checkbox;;
val values : Silica_ui.Story.values = <abstr>
# Silica_ui.Story.string values "label", Silica_ui.Story.bool values "checked";;
- : string * bool = ("Accept the terms", false)
# let values = Silica_ui.Story.set_bool values "checked" true;;
val values : Silica_ui.Story.values = <abstr>
# Silica_ui.Story.bool values "checked";;
- : bool = true
# Silica_ui.Story.slug Doc_stories.checkbox;;
- : string = "checkbox"
]}

[Silica_ui.Story.controls] draws the controls of a story with silica-ui: a
switch for each toggle and a select for each choice. Each change gives the new
values to a function, which makes an event. A parent machine keeps one value
of [values] for each story, and gives it to [render].

{1:browser The component browser}

The site keeps a catalog: for each component, its name, a summary and its
stories. The page of a component is a machine that keeps the values of each
story. For each story it shows the rendered story, its controls and its
source. It also renders the first story in each shipped theme side by side,
with [Silica_ui.Theme.scope]. [Story.slug] gives the anchor of each story.

An app can make the same page for its own components with a list of stories,
a machine that keeps their values, and [Silica_ui.Story.render] and
[Silica_ui.Story.controls] in the view.
